\DOC LOCAL_CMSUB38_P521_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_CMSUB38_P521_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Discharges one inlined subroutine block of a p521 point-doubling proof,
advancing the symbolic-execution goal past the block's code and recording the
block's arithmetic specification as a new assumption.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument, an {int}, selects which code/offset variant of the
enclosing program is being verified: it determines the entry/exit
program-counter offsets and the register/memory frame that is matched. The
second, a {string list}, names the result variable followed by the input
variables of the block; these fix the names used in the newly added arithmetic
assumption and in the memory-read assumption for the output buffer. Applied to
an {eventually} goal about state {s}, the tactic produces a single goal whose
conclusion is unchanged except that the state variable becomes a fresh later
state:
{
   A ?- eventually x86 (\s'. P s') s
   ================================================  LOCAL_CMSUB38_P521_TAC n ["r"; "x1"; ...; "xn"]
   A' ?- eventually x86 (\s'. P s') s'
}
\noindent In {A'}, every assumption of {A} mentioning the old state — the
program counter value, {bytes_loaded}, register and memory reads, and the
accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame — is removed and re-asserted for the new state,
with the program-counter assumption now giving the block's exit address and the
{MAYCHANGE} frame extended by the component written by the block. A new
memory-read assumption equates that written component with the named result
variable, and a further implication assumption is added stating, under range
hypotheses on the named inputs, the modular-arithmetic value of the named
result. Assumptions of {A} that do not mention the state are kept, possibly
reordered.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-goal, state-advancing behaviour, on
goals of this one {eventually}-style shape with a large assumption list
describing an x86 machine state.

\ENDDOC
